---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';

const path = '/for/supplements';
const title = 'Shopify cart recovery for supplement stores';
const desc = 'Answer product questions without inferring a shopper’s health needs.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="container py-16 md:py-24">
<a class="link" href="/for">Explore related guides</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Shopify cart recovery for supplement stores</h1>
<p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">Answer product questions without inferring a shopper’s health needs.</p>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Use product facts, not a diagnosis</h2>
<p>Lead with the exact selected formulation, pack size, ingredient list and current label. Do not turn browsing or cart contents into a claim that the shopper has a condition. Avoid sensitive health inferences in segments, subject lines and message previews. Route personal suitability questions to a qualified health professional rather than an automated sales answer.</p>
<h2>Review claims at the message level</h2>
<p>For US context, <a class="link" href="https://www.fda.gov/food/information-consumers-using-dietary-supplements/questions-and-answers-dietary-supplements">FDA’s supplement Q&amp;A</a> explains that FDA does not approve supplements before marketing and advises consumers to consult a health professional about use. Do not add an FDA-approved badge or disease-treatment promise to recovery copy. Have the responsible reviewer assess product claims and linked testimonials for the actual market; this guide does not establish compliance.</p>
<h2>Distinguish purchase type and formulation</h2>
<p>A one-time pack and a subscription can share a product image while having different commitments. Preserve selling plan, quantity, introductory terms and subsequent billing wording. Do not assume a universal consumption interval or infer that someone needs a refill from an abandoned cart. An ingredient or strength change requires an accurate product choice, not a silent substitution.</p>
<h2>Choose an education path before an incentive</h2>
<p>Answer a documented question with the relevant label or product information. Avoid a countdown that pressures someone to decide about suitability. Coordinate cart recovery with existing subscription service messages so a current subscriber is not offered a duplicate plan. Include support access for label and ordering questions.</p>
<h2>Proposed acceptance exercise</h2>
<p>Queue two pack sizes and a subscription version, revise a product label, and inspect the queued message and return link. Check formulation, plan, claims approval and purchase exit. Ask the reviewer to inspect testimonial blocks as well as the main copy. These exercises have not been performed.</p>
<h2>Evaluate the required data</h2>
<p>Require demonstrated SKU and selling-plan fidelity, approved-content controls and an exclusion record. Compare <a class="link" href="/for/food-and-beverage">food shipping constraints</a> and <a class="link" href="/for/subscription-boxes">box billing and gifts</a>; supplement suitability and claim review remain distinct from fulfillment and assortment choices.</p>
</article>
</section>
</BaseLayout>
